WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.089 --> 00:00:10.330
This fresh, juicy, pulpy red ball is a part
of most of the delicacies that we relish

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:11.390
while eating.

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:17.320
But can you tell me whether a tomato is a
vegetable or a fruit?

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:22.070
Some say it is a fruit, while others say it
is a vegetable!

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.710
And which is the right
answer?

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.390
Let us find that out!

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:31.660
To know this, we must first know what exactly
a fruit is!

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:36.480
For a lay person, it is
simply a pulpy sweet edible part of a plant.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.250
But a botanist will define it
precisely in a different way!

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:44.450
A fruit is defined as a seed bearing structure
which

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.910
is actually the ripened or mature ovary developed
post fertilisation.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:56.280
So in a typical flower, after fertilisation,
the petals wear off, this part, that is

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.360
the ovary, develops into the pulpy fruit,
and these ovules into seeds.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.010
So this is how a typical fruit appears to
us!

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:07.010
Right?

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:08.010
Yes!

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.859
This is how it looks when cut!

00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:15.939
Now if we were to label this fruit, we would
basically divide it in two major parts.

00:01:15.939 --> 00:01:22.030
This central region, as the seed, and the
surrounding pulpy region as the Pericarp.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:27.820
The seed mainly has the embryo, which is located
here.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.880
This is surrounded by
the nourishing tissue called endosperm.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.649
And this complete unit is surrounded
by a tough covering called the seed coat meant

00:01:36.649 --> 00:01:38.740
mostly for protection.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.880
The pericarp on the other hand is the edible
part.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:49.560
It consists of the outermost,
thin, epicarp or also called the exocarp.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:56.009
The word originates from the Greek
words “epi” meaning above or upon and

00:01:56.009 --> 00:01:57.789
“carp” meaning fruit.

00:01:57.789 --> 00:02:01.950
So this is the
outermost layer present on the fruit.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.150
Next is the middle layer called Mesocarp.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:10.340
The term “meso” in Greek means “middle”
and “carp” as we know is “fruit”.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:14.569
This is usually pulpy or juicy in most of
the fruits.

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:19.790
So it is mainly this part which
is consumed by animals when a fruit is eaten!

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.740
Lastly, we have the “endocarp”.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.540
The term “Endo” means “within” or
“inside”.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.690
So it is the last layer of the
Pericarp which surrounds the seed.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:38.170
Depending upon the fruit, endocarps can
be either membranous or thick.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:40.740
These were the parts of a typical fruit.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:44.879
Now tell me, do all fruits have this kind
of structure only?

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:47.250
Do they all look similar?

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:48.760
No, right?

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.980
Fruits like pineapple,
berries, almonds, and melons….

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.370
All of these look completely different.

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:59.390
So don’t
these have the layers that we just discussed?

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:00.690
Of course they do!

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.819
Yet these
fruits are different from each other!

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:07.310
That means fruits come in various types!

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:11.769
So let us understand the various types of
fruits in detail.

00:03:11.769 --> 00:03:15.219
Classification of fruits can be done in a
variety of ways.

00:03:15.219 --> 00:03:19.430
We will have a look at
the two major ways of classifying fruits.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:24.430
The first basis is on the parameter of
the morphology of the fruit.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.499
And the second parameter would be the part
of

00:03:26.499 --> 00:03:29.430
the plant from where the fruit develops.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:33.319
Let us begin with each, one by one in the
next part!

